    <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition">
      <body>
        <div rend="parallel">
            <p xml:id="Aink433-kilavi">pirivu uṭampaṭṭum āṟṟāyākiṉṟatu eṉṉai eṉṟu viṉaviyavaḻi avar pōṉa curam pōkaṟku aritu eṉṟu āṟṟēṉ ākiṉṟēṉ eṉṟa talaimakaṭku vēṉiṟkālam kaḻintatu; kārpparuvattōṟṟattilē pirintār ākalāṉ accuram naṉṟu eṉat tōḻi colli āṟṟuvittatu.</p>
            <p xml:lang="en">After asking, 'And having agreed to the separation, why is it that you have become unable to endure [it]?', to the heroine who said, 'Thinking of the wasteland through which he goes that is difficult to traverse, I have become unable to endure [it]', so as to say, 'The hot season has passed; because he left when the rainy season appeared, that wasteland is good', the confidante reassured [the heroine] by speaking [this].<note place="foot" xml:lang="en">This <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">kiḷavi</foreign> is appended to the previous poem, 431, but there is a note that it belongs to the whole decad. For the reader's convenience, it is provided here, although it is not in fact present in the edition.</note></p>
              </div>
        <div rend="parallel">
          <lg xml:id="Aink433">        
            <l>naṉṟē kātalar ceṉṟa vāṟē</l>
            <l>nīrppaṭa veḻili vīcuṅ</l>
            <l>kārppeyaṟ ketiriya kāṉamu muṭaittē.</l>
        </lg>  
 <lg xml:lang="en">                    
    <l>The way along which the lover went is good --</l>
<l>there are forests too that have met the monsoon rains</l>
<l>which the clouds fling down so that water appears.<note xml:lang="en" place="foot"><foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">nīrppaṭa</foreign> can either be construed as a resultative infinitive, as in the present translation, in which case I think it is likely that it refers to water appearing in the forest, perhaps gathered in pools on the ground. Alternatively, one could also take it as a temporal infintive, where it could either describe the act of water occurring in the cloud or on the ground. If on the ground, then there is very negligable difference to the current translation. If in the cloud, then the final line would read: 'which, as water occurred [in the clouds], the clouds fling down.' </note></l>
</lg>                      
        </div>
      </body>
    </text>
